<figure id="perfect-substitutes-diagram">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 620 430"
    xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="perfect-substitutes-title"
  >
    <title id="perfect-substitutes-title">Perfectly Substitutable Inputs</title>

    <!-- Axes -->
    <line class="axis" x1="75" y1="35" x2="75" y2="355"></line>
    <line class="axis" x1="75" y1="355" x2="565" y2="355"></line>

    <!-- Axis labels -->
    <text class="axis-label" x="75" y="22" text-anchor="middle">
      Capital (K)
    </text>

    <text class="axis-label" x="565" y="385" text-anchor="end">
      Labour (L)
    </text>

    <!-- Straight isoquant -->
    <line class="isoquant" x1="135" y1="85" x2="500" y2="325"></line>

    <!-- Points -->
    <circle class="point" cx="225" cy="145" r="5"></circle>
    <circle class="point" cx="365" cy="235" r="5"></circle>

    <!-- Point labels -->
    <text class="point-label" x="210" y="135"> A </text>

    <text class="point-label" x="375" y="230"> B </text>

    <!-- Isoquant label -->
    <text class="curve-label" x="310" y="185"> Q₁ </text>
  </svg>

  <figcaption>
    <strong>Constant MRTS.</strong>
    With perfectly substitutable inputs, capital and labour can replace each other
    at a constant rate, so the isoquant is a straight line.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  #perfect-substitutes-diagram {
    max-width: 620px;
